  },
  "articleBody": "It’s really frustrating to see cracks appearing in your newly drywalled room addition. There are several reasons this can happen, and understanding them helps in knowing when to get professional help. One common culprit is uneven framing. If the wall studs or ceiling joists weren’t perfectly straight or plumb when the drywall was installed, stresses can build up, leading to cracks, especially around seams. Another big factor is movement. New additions can settle a bit over time, or if the new structure isn’t properly integrated with the existing house, differential movement can occur, causing drywall to crack. Skipping proper prep work before installation, like not adequately taping and mudding joints, also leaves the drywall vulnerable to cracking under normal stress. Sometimes, it’s simply a matter of the drywall being installed before the building was fully enclosed and climate-controlled, allowing for expansion and contraction that causes issues. Lastly, if the rough-in inspections for plumbing or electrical weren't completed before drywall, potential issues behind the walls could also contribute to cracking. If you're seeing persistent or widening cracks, particularly around doors, windows, or corners, it's definitely time to get an experienced drywall and finish contractor to take a look. They can diagnose the underlying issue and ensure a lasting repair.",
